{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794382282440,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c984beb1001a9iBmtTp28d5Ms","messageID":"msg_1c984a561001O6PGFr3igZdzZK","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794382282440,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c984beb4001T4rx7FWL6eKkHd","messageID":"msg_1c984a561001O6PGFr3igZdzZK","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file has hashes, and adding itsdangerous to requirements.txt without a hash breaks `--require-hashes` mode. The lock file is a constraints file with hashes; pip uses hashes from the constraints file? Apparently not for the constraint file entries.\n\nOptions:\n1. Remove itsdangerous from requirements.txt (it's a transitive dependency of Flask anyway, guaranteed present). The README says requirements.txt holds dependencies; itsdangerous comes with Flask. Since Flask pins itsdangerous>=2.2, and the lock pins 2.2.0, it's fine.\n2. Add a hash line for itsdangerous to requirements.txt.\n\nCleanest: keep requirements.txt as flask + gunicorn (itsdangerous is a Flask dependency, and the lock file constrains it to 2.2.0). But explicitly listing a directly-imported dependency is better practice. Let me add the hash for itsdangerous==2.2.0 to requirements.txt. I need the sha256 hash of the itsdangerous 2.2.0 sdist/wheel. Let me check the lock file for the itsdangerous hashes.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794382282420,"end":1794382282437}}}
