{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794382288048,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c984d4a1001ApWg3JpiR3ts6B","messageID":"msg_1c984bed7001fy2ygbKdWBSIvj","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794382288048,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c984d4a3001gBBUXAGjBm145e","messageID":"msg_1c984bed7001fy2ygbKdWBSIvj","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file has hashes for itsdangerous. But since pip's --require-hashes mode requires hashes in the *requirements* file being installed (requirements.txt), the lock as a constraints file doesn't supply hashes.\n\nOptions:\n- Keep requirements.txt without itsdangerous (it's installed via flask's dependency, constrained by the lock to 2.2.0). Simple, and the Docker install works as before.\n\nSince the README states requirements.lock is used as a pip constraints file, and flask requires itsdangerous>=2.2, the package will be installed. I'll remove itsdangerous from requirements.txt to avoid breaking the hashed install.\n\nHmm, but wait: does the hashed lock's \"via flask\" comment still hold? Yes, itsdangerous is required by flask.\n\nLet me revert requirements.txt.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794382288035,"end":1794382288046}}}
