{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794382292133,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c984e497001fqhBRahsdeKszj","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_1c984d4bf001qP2UtUNIbGwyxK","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":48234,"input":127,"output":107,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":48000}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794382301075,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c985077f0016e77rpfIbjABH1","messageID":"msg_1c984e4a0001e2jYexsPjMa0gv","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794382301075,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c9850782001jzx37DQWGjrFRb","messageID":"msg_1c984e4a0001e2jYexsPjMa0gv","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"text","text":"<think>Hmm, this fails even with the original requirements.txt (unchanged). So the local pip install with the constraints file fails due to hash requirements — probably because the constraint file's hashes aren't used for the top-level requirement. Wait, but the Dockerfile does exactly this:\n\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\n\nIf that fails, the Docker build would fail! Let me check: the error says flask==3.1.3 needs a hash in requirements.txt. But the lock has flask hashes. In pip, when a constraints file contains hashes, pip requires hashes for the packages... Actually the constraint file's hashes DO apply if the constraint pins the exact version? Hmm, pip's --require-hashes mode activates when any requirement (including constraints?) has a hash. The lock file's entries have hashes → mode on → the top-level requirement flask==3.1.3 (from requirements.txt) has no hash → error.\n\nWait, but pip 25.1.1 might behave differently from the pip in the Docker image (python:3.13-slim, likely pip 25.x too). Hmm, this would mean the provided Dockerfile is broken?! Let me test the exact Docker command to see.\n\nActually, hold on: the error message says \"The following packages were requested with --require-hashes ...\" Let me just try installing without the constraints file, and separately test the Docker build later.\n\nMaybe the issue is the constraint file uses a format where hashes are attached with \"\\\" line continuations, and pip needs the constraint to be a \"hash-checking mode\" source. Let me look at the lock file's beginning to see the header.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794382301058,"end":1794382301071}}}
