{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794382315470,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c9853fb3001OSpvpjN6LHw1WH","messageID":"msg_1c98507aa001fOZBmKMgzxtsNM","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794382315471,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c9853fb6001uAZ7y8CGYp93b3","messageID":"msg_1c98507aa001fOZBmKMgzxtsNM","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file is a standard pip-compile output with hashes. The Docker command is:\n\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\n\nThe issue: pip's hash-checking mode is enabled when *any* requirement has a hash — including in the constraints file? The error message indicates hash mode was on. But the Dockerfile's command has been given as the \"run contract\" — maybe the Docker build works because pip in the Docker image behaves differently, or maybe pip resolves the constraint's hashes onto the top-level requirement.\n\nLet me test the exact command in a fresh venv to see whether it's my local pip version or a general issue. It's the same command. It failed. Hmm, but maybe it failed because my local pip is 25.1.1 while the image has a newer pip (25.3+?) that handles constraints hashes correctly.\n\nLet me test with `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock --dry-run` and also try without the constraints file to see if the plain install works. Let me check pip versions: python:3.13-slim currently ships pip 25.x. Hmm.\n\nActually — important: pip's behavior. In pip, `--require-hashes` is enabled automatically if ANY requirement in the *requirements file* has a hash. Requirements in a *constraints* file with hashes... Let me check pip's source: in `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/candidates.py`... Actually in `pip/_internal/req/req_install.py`, `RequirementPreparer`... The relevant code is in `pip/_internal/operations/prepare.py`? I recall: \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements\" — triggered when `self.require_hashes` is True. `require_hashes` is set in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n\n```python\nif any(req.is_hash_allowed...)  # hmm\n```\nActually:\n```python\n    def get_requirements(self):\n        ...\n        for req in requirements:\n            req.is_direct = True\n            ...\n        self.require_hashes = any(req.hash_options for req in requirements)\n```\nHmm, that's for the requirements passed via -r. But constraints are also parsed and merged into requirements via `get_requirements`? In `RequirementCommand.get_requirements`, constraints are collected separately:\n\n```python\n    def get_requirements(self) -> List[InstallRequirement]:\n        ...\n        for req in requirements:\n            ...\n```\nand then in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n```python\n        self._add_pip_requirements...\n```\nHmm. The error we saw lists flask==3.1.3 with its actual downloaded hash, and says hashes are missing for some requirements — meaning pip was in require_hashes mode. Since requirements.txt has no hashes, the mode must have been enabled by the constraints file. So pip does enable hash mode from constraints too (in newer pip versions).\n\nSo: does the Docker build fail? If pip in the image behaves the same → yes, it fails → the whole app can't build! That would be a serious issue with the provided scaffold. But the task says \"The app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root\" — presumably the scaffold was tested. Hmm, but maybe the Docker image's pip version is newer and behaves differently (e.g., pip 25.2+ supports hash-checking constraints properly).\n\nLet me check what pip version python:3.13-slim has now (Nov 2026): probably pip 25.3 or 26.x. Newer pip may correctly apply constraint hashes to the pinned requirement.\n\nI can't run Docker here (probably not available). Let me check if docker is available.\n\nAlternatively, to be safe, I can make the Dockerfile install with `--require-hashes` using the lock file as the requirements file (not constraints). I.e.:\n\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock\n```\nThat installs exactly the locked packages with hash verification — and the lock includes flask, gunicorn and all transitive deps. But the README says requirements.txt holds dependencies and requirements.lock is used as a constraints file. If I change the Dockerfile, I should keep the README accurate.\n\nHmm, but maybe I shouldn't touch the Dockerfile if it works. Let me first test locally with the exact pip version in the image. Let me check if docker is available in the sandbox.\n\nLet me check `docker --version` and also try `pip download`/`pip install` with the newer pip via a pip upgrade in my venv to see behavior differences.\n\nActually, a simpler test: create a fresh venv, upgrade pip to the latest, then run the exact command.\n\nLet me do that. But first, check docker availability.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794382315446,"end":1794382315466}}}
