{"type":"text","timestamp":1794382335047,"sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","part":{"id":"prt_1c9858c32001e1XadY3Z1dCwRk","messageID":"msg_1c9856f6e001gAtI4iXDNJmMmU","sessionID":"ses_e3683fd44ffeu9mw9DmUwiGHZw","type":"text","text":"<think>With pip 26.3 (which is what python:3.13-slim likely ships as of Nov 2026), the Docker install command works fine. The old pip 25.1.1 in my system Python failed, but the image's pip is newer.\n\nSo .venv2 has the packages installed. Let me use .venv2 for testing. Let me remove .venv (the failed one) and keep .venv2. Actually let me just use .venv2 and delete .venv at the end.\n\nNow let me write a test script to exercise the API. I'll create it in /workspace? Better to keep the repo clean — I'll put the test script in /tmp/opencode (not writable!). Hmm, /tmp/opencode is root-owned and not writable by node. Let me check if /tmp itself is writable (sticky bit, yes: drwxrwxrwt). I can write to /tmp directly (e.g., /tmp/test_api.py). The bash tool's file writing should use the Write tool with an absolute path... but external directory rules might block /tmp except /tmp/opencode/*. Let me test writing to /tmp/test_api.py via the Write tool. Actually the bash policy mentions \"/tmp/opencode\" for temporary work, but it's root-owned. Let me check whether I can create files in /tmp with bash (as node user, /tmp is world-writable → yes).\n\nI'll write the test script to /workspace/.tmp-tests/ and delete it later, or just write it to /tmp via bash heredoc... but I'm told to avoid using bash for file writes; use the Write tool. Let me try Write to /tmp/api_test.py.\n\nActually, simplest: keep the test script inside the workspace at /workspace/scripts/smoke_test.py — hmm, it adds a file to the repo. The task doesn't prohibit adding a test script, but graders might not care. I'd rather keep the repo clean and delete it after. Let me write to /tmp/api_test.py with the Write tool and see if it's allowed.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1